# Git / PR workflow

## The bleeding-edge cycle

`bleeding-edge` is the standing integration branch (every repo of the
owner's has `main` plus `bleeding-edge`). It always carries main + every
open PR branch + work in progress, so the owner's running app always has the
latest code regardless of merge status.

1. Day-to-day work accumulates **uncommitted** in the working tree on
   `bleeding-edge`. Do not commit or push it — not after a feature, not
   after a review round.
2. The owner tests and iterates through feedback rounds, still uncommitted.
3. Only when he says to slice: cut the work into PRs targeting `main`
   (branch from `origin/main`, apply the relevant subset, commit, push,
   `gh pr create`).
4. Merge the PR branches back into `bleeding-edge` (merge, never rebase —
   same for open PR tips and `main` whenever they move), then push
   `bleeding-edge` (only after the PRs exist, never before).

## Slicing discipline

- Never commit on `main`.
- One PR = one coherent group of related changes, 150–400 LOC. Meta files
  (rules, docs) ride a dedicated docs PR, not feature PRs.
- The commit message IS the PR body: motivation paragraph, `## Changes`,
  `## Test plan`. No em dashes.
- Fix review feedback in place on the PR branch (new commit + push), never
  as a fixup PR stacked on top.

## The owner merges fast and out of order

He reviews and merges PRs on GitHub within minutes, in any order, sometimes
into other PRs' base branches. Before mutating any branch: `git fetch` and
check actual PR states with `gh pr view` — the local picture is often
stale. After his merges, fast-forward `main`, re-merge into
`bleeding-edge`, and rebuild any still-open slices on the new base rather
than force-pushing amended history over merged work.
